INT. ST. BRIDGET'S CHURCH BASEMENT - NIGHT

Folding chairs in a circle on cracked linoleum. A coffee urn sputters. Under a flickering tube light, six people sit with paper cups, and one of them is wearing a wire.

Dana Whitlock, 30s, sharp-eyed, an undercover agent in a thrift-store cardigan, sits beside Walter Pruitt, 60s, a retired accountant who hasn't touched his coffee, while across the circle Rosalind Kerr, 50s, a lawyer with ink-stained fingers, grips the arm of Tomas Reyes, 20s, who bounces his knee, and Father Gil Moran, 70s, the host, folds his hands as Priya Anand, 40s, a nurse in scrubs, steps in late and closes the door too carefully.

CLOSE ON Dana's cup. A pinhole lens in the rim.

DANA
I'm Dana. I'm an alcoholic.

ALL
Hi, Dana.

ANGLE ON Walter. He watches her hands, not her face.

This is the last time any of them will sit in this room together.

WALTER
You sound new.

DANA
Three weeks.

WALTER
Funny. You smell like gun oil.

Silence. The urn gurgles. Rosalind's grip on Tomas tightens.

INSERT - Priya's phone, face-down on her knee. A text lights the screen: HE KNOWS.

WE SEE Father Moran slowly stand, one hand sliding into his cassock.

BACK TO SCENE

TOMAS
Nobody move.

FATHER MORAN
Sit down, son. Everyone sit. Dana, the cup, please.

Dana sets it on the floor. Slowly.
